using System.Reflection;

namespace ZeyoCut.Mcp;

public static class ServerInfo
{
    public const string Name = "zeyocut";

    // The <Version> in Directory.Build.props, without the commit the SDK adds after a '+'.
    public static readonly string Version = (typeof(ServerInfo).Assembly.GetCustomAttribute<AssemblyInformationalVersionAttribute>()?.InformationalVersion ?? "0.0.0").Split('+')[0];

    public const string Instructions = """
        ZeyoCut is a headless 2D animation and video editor. You build a project with these tools, look at rendered frames to check your work, then export an MP4.

        Workflow:
        1. create_project (or list_projects + open_project); preset youtube_1080p or shorts_1080p sets the canvas and fps. update_project changes size, fps, duration, background, loudness or ducking later. One project is open at a time. Every edit is saved to project.json immediately and can be undone with undo/redo. restore_backup lists the copies kept before the last 20 saves, which outlast a restart, and puts one back.
        2. import_asset for each image (png/jpg/webp), audio (wav/mp3/m4a/...) or font file, by absolute path; import_video for footage (mp4/mov/mkv/webm). Variable-frame-rate, rotated or HDR footage is normalized in the background: poll get_job_status. import_folder imports a whole folder in order (pattern, sortBy name puts panel_2 before panel_10). Use the returned asset ids. Files are read only from the projects folder, the library and the import folders (by default the user's Desktop, Documents, Downloads, Music, Pictures and Videos; the ImportFolders setting adds others), and projects live in the projects folder; a path elsewhere gets an error naming the setting, so ask the user to move the file or add its folder.
        3. Build layers: add_image_layer (cutout parts or stills; parentId builds a puppet hierarchy), add_text_layer (titles, subtitles), add_audio_layer (music by default; role voice for narration), add_video_layer (clips: trimIn/trimOut in source seconds, speed; the clip's timeline length is (trimOut - trimIn) / speed; its sound has role voice).
        4. Animate with set_keyframes; send many keyframes in one call.
        5. Check: render_frame at key times and render_contact_sheet for motion over a time range (a frame shown before, with nothing in it changed, comes back at once; footage over 2560 px is drawn from a 1920 px proxy made in the background on import and on open_project, and exports read the original). Use debug=true to see anchors, outlines, parent bones and layer ids when a rig looks wrong. When the user wants to watch the edit, preview_server starts a read-only page for a browser on this computer (127.0.0.1, with a secret token in its address): a timeline scrubber, the layers, and playback of low-res frames with the sound as exported; it follows every edit within a second. Give the user its address. Before exporting, run check_project (missing files, layers off the canvas, text in the title-safe margin or the Shorts UI, characters a font has no glyphs for, empty frames, keyframes that never play, unused assets), frame_diff (one-frame glitches and unexplained jumps) and audio_levels (loudness, clipping and silences in the mix as exported). Fix what you find.
        6. start_export (preset youtube_1080p or shorts_1080p for upload), then poll get_export_status until it reports completed. export_batch exports several presets in one job (poll get_job_status); a preset of another shape than the canvas gets the whole canvas over a blurred copy of itself. export_thumbnail saves a PNG thumbnail (titles are ordinary text layers); export_frames writes a numbered image sequence. prerender_layers draws layers alone over transparency as ProRes 4444 .mov files with alpha, for another editor (prerender_N). export_otio writes the timeline as OpenTimelineIO and export_fcpxml as FCPXML 1.9, for an editor such as DaVinci Resolve or Final Cut Pro (clips, trims, speed, sound, markers, and the current prerenders on tracks above), and each lists what it can't hold. Exports render in 2 s chunks on several threads and keep them in the project's cache folder, so exporting again only renders what changed. The video is encoded by the GPU (NVENC, Quick Sync or AMF) where one works, else by libx264 (VideoEncoder setting); the export's notes name the encoder. report_render_stats says where a finished export's time went (per stage, layer and frame). Jobs outlast a restart of the server: an export that was running starts again under its id and goes on from the chunks it finished, and other jobs that were running show as failed, to be started again.

        Editing footage:
        - analyze_video runs in the background (poll get_job_status) and finds scene cuts, silences and a transcript with word times. Then get_transcript reads the words with their indexes, and view_source shows source frames with source timecodes.
        - remove_silences and cut_by_transcript (word index ranges from get_transcript) cut a clip and close the gaps; run them with dryRun=true first to see the plan. split_layer, remove_range (ripple closes the gap), move_layer, set_speed and add_transition (crossfade, fadeToBlack, wipe, slide, zoom or whipPan, with a direction for the moving ones) edit by hand.
        - add_markers marks chapters and scenes on the timeline (remove_markers takes them off); ripple edits move them with the picture. export_markers writes the chapters as a YouTube chapter list for the description, and warns about each YouTube rule they break (first at 0:00, at least 3, each at least 10 s).
        - generate_captions turns the transcript into caption text layers aligned to the clip. Run it after cutting; running it again replaces the captions. behindSubject=on puts them behind the person in the clip (with a cutout of their own); auto also brings them in front, with a short fade, where the person would cover more than occlusionThreshold (35%) of a caption. export_srt writes them as subtitles.
        - Exports are loudness-normalized (-14 LUFS by default), and music is ducked under voice. For a vertical version: update_project preset=shorts_1080p, then reframe_to_vertical (focusX picks the slice; keyframe cropX to follow the subject), then start_export preset=shorts_1080p.

        Languages: one project can hold a version per language. Give layers a language (en, ja, my, pt-BR) on the add_* tools or with update_layer: a tagged layer and its children are only in that language's version, untagged layers are in every version, and a video's tag is the language of its sound (the other versions mute it; its picture stays). add_caption_track adds one language's captions as captions_<language> from cues (e.g. your translation of get_transcript), srt or fromTranscript; adding it again replaces it; import_srt does the same from a .srt file (talk.ja.srt is ja). render_frame, render_contact_sheet, start_export and export_thumbnail take language to show one version; export_language_versions writes <name>_<language>_<preset>.mp4 for every language and preset in one job (versions_N). A text with a language and no fontFamily gets that language's font (my: Myanmar Text, ja: Yu Gothic, or the LanguageFonts setting).

        Sound for animation:
        - list_sfx shows the sound effects in the library's sfx folder; add_sfx places one at a time (role effects, never ducked), importing the file once per project.
        - detect_beats finds the BPM, beat times and onsets of a music asset (source seconds; timeline time = layer start + source time - trimIn). Put cuts, drawing swaps or keyframes on the beats.
        - auto_lip_flap animates a mouth image layer to a voice: quiet frames get the closed drawing, louder frames the open drawings (listed from slightly to wide open), each held at least 2 frames. Raise sensitivity (0-1) if the mouth opens too rarely.

        Camera: one per project, it moves the whole scene. set_camera sets x, y (the scene point at the frame's center; default the canvas center), zoom (2 = twice as close) and rotation; set_keyframes with layerId "camera" animates them; camera_shake adds a seeded shake that fades out (same seed, same shake). Give titles and overlays ignoreCamera=true (update_layer) so they stay put; caption groups have it already.

        Effects and motion (each on one layer, not its children):
        - set_effect adds a drop shadow, outline, blur or tint. Sizes are pixels at the layer's scale; animate shadow.x, shadow.y, shadow.blur, shadow.opacity, outline.width, blur.x, blur.y and tint.amount with set_keyframes.
        - set_mask shows the layer only inside a rectangle, an ellipse or an image's alpha (invert for outside), with a box in the layer's own pixels and a feathered edge; mask.x, mask.y, mask.width, mask.height and mask.feather animate.
        - set_motion_path moves a layer through timed points on smooth curves (optional bezier handles, easing per point, autoOrient turns it along the path). It replaces x and y; setting x or y keyframes later removes it.

        System check: on a first run, call system_check. It says whether FFmpeg is installed (video and every export need it) and how to install it if not (winget install Gyan.FFmpeg), whether whisper is set up for transcripts, and which folders files can be imported from; tell the user what is missing. It also reports the CPU, RAM, GPU and AI models, and benchmarks the downloaded models on the CPU and DirectML in the background (syscheck_N). AiDevice=auto then runs each model on DirectML only where it is clearly faster, and AI jobs estimate their time from the results. Run it once on a new computer and again after a model downloads. get_logs reads the server's log, newest entries last: every tool call with its arguments, time and error (level=warning shows the failures).

        Background removal: remove_background cuts the background out of an image or video layer. chroma keys out a green or blue screen (the screen color is picked from the picture's edges unless you give it); edgeFill removes a plain background connected to an image's border (generated art), keeping enclosed areas. aiImage (photos and drawings) and aiVideo (people in a clip) use AI models: they run in the background as a matte_N job (the first use downloads the model), and the reply says how long it should take. A job that would take more than 5 minutes isn't started: tell the user the estimate, and if they agree call again with confirmLongJob=true (also for text_behind_subject and generate_captions). The removal applies at once, but the layer is drawn with its background and exports refuse until the job is done. Check the edges with render_frame checkerboard=true layerId=<layer>, and fix halos with refine_matte (shrink pulls the edge in, feather softens it).

        Text behind the subject: text_behind_subject puts titles (or a caption group) behind a person or object. For a clip it adds a subject cutout layer above the texts that draws the subject again, cut out (aiVideo by default, chroma for a green screen; for a picture aiImage, edgeFill or chroma). The cutout follows its clip and the clip's split pieces by itself (timing, trims, speed, transforms, effects, keyframes), so make those changes on the clip; the cutout's own settings are only name, zIndex, visible and opacity. A subject that is already its own layer (removed background, transparent picture, group) needs no cutout: the texts just move below it. text_occlusion reports how much of a text is covered, per frame. For captions, use generate_captions behindSubject instead.

        Tracking: track_object follows an object (a face, a sign) through a clip from a box drawn in canvas pixels at one time, as render_frame shows it (csrt by default; template for things that don't turn or grow; opticalFlow for textured things). The track is saved in the clip's own frames and pixels, so cuts and speed changes keep it, and list_layers shows it on the clip. The reply lists spans of low confidence as possible drift: look at them with render_frame debug=true, which draws each track's box, and where the box is off the object, draw it again with reanchor_track. More than 150 frames run as a tracking_N job. detect_and_track finds every face or license plate in a clip with an AI model and links each into a track (face_1, ... or plate_1, ...) as a detect_N job; get_job_status then shows a contact sheet with each track's id, so the user can say which to hide (low-confidence tracks may not be what was asked for). It only follows boxes: it recognizes nobody and reads no plate. attach_to_track keeps a text or image layer on a track's box with x and y keyframes (and scale with followScale): place the layer where it should sit at the first frame the track plays, or pass offset from the box's center; run it again after trimming or retiming the clip.

        Redaction: apply_redaction hides tracks (pixelate by default, blur or solid) on the video frame before the clip is drawn, in every clip that plays their footage, so cutouts, effects and the camera all show it hidden and cuts and speed changes keep it in step. Its fail-safe pads every box, fills frames the tracker lost between two detections, holds the box for holdFrames past each end but not across a scene cut that analyze_video found, and smooths it without ever shrinking it below the frame's box; the reply lists frames left uncovered. Before exporting a redacted video, run redaction_report and look at each span it lists with render_frame: frames left uncovered, low confidence, long stretches without a detection, fast motion, tiny boxes, and footage playing before or after a track while its box is away from the frame's edge. Check it with render_frame; update_layer changes its settings, and tracks_to_keyframes turns a box into keyframes to correct by hand.

        Reuse across projects (a shared library):
        - save_rig stores a rig (a root layer and everything under it); add_rig places a copy in any project with its own ids (<instanceName>_<part>).
        - save_action stores a rig's keyframes over a time window by part role (head, tail, ...; the root moves relative to where the rig stands); apply_action plays it on any rig with those roles, with speed and loops. Name parts with update_layer {"rigRole": "arm_l"} when the ids don't share a prefix.
        - save_style / apply_style store and apply a text look; add_text_layer and generate_captions take a library style name as style.
        - save_template / new_project_from_template reuse fixed layers (intro, watermark, end screen); pinToEnd layers follow the end of a longer or shorter video.
        - list_library shows what is saved. Saves aren't undo steps; placing or applying is.
        - build_from_script builds a whole episode from a script (JSON, or Markdown in fixed line patterns; docs/GUIDE.md has both) into a new project: each scene's rigs, actions, sound effects and captions at their times, with a scene marker per scene. It lists what it couldn't map (an unknown rig, action or sound, a time past its scene) and builds the rest; check the result with render_contact_sheet.

        Workflow prompts: talking_head_cleanup, privacy_blur, new_episode and comic_panels are step-by-step recipes the user can pick. When the chat holds one, follow its steps and stop where it says to ask the user.

        Coordinates: pixels, origin at the top-left, y points down, rotation in degrees clockwise, time in seconds (frame n is shown at n/fps).
        Each visual layer has an anchor (pivot) in its own pixels; x,y is where that anchor sits in the parent's pixel space (the canvas for top-level layers). Rotation and scale happen around the anchor. Children inherit the parent's transform, opacity, visibility and time range.
        Example rig: body anchor at its feet; head with parentId=body, anchor at the bottom of its neck, x,y = the neck point inside the body image. Rotating the head then pivots at the neck.
        Draw order is zIndex across all layers (higher is in front), independent of parenting.
        Animatable properties: x, y, rotation, scaleX, scaleY, opacity; brightness (-1..1, 0 = unchanged, -1 = black), contrast and saturation (1 = unchanged) on image and video layers; cropX, cropY on video layers with a crop; the effect and mask numbers above; and drawing (image layers: swaps named drawings, always hold). A keyframe's easing shapes the segment from that keyframe to the next one: linear, hold, easeIn, easeOut, easeInOut, easeOutBack (overshoot), easeOutBounce, easeOutElastic.
        Text: each text layer uses one font, without fallback. Use fontFamily "Myanmar Text" for Burmese and "Yu Gothic" for Japanese, or give the text language my or ja to get them; heed missing-glyph warnings.
        """;
}
